+\noindent\textbf{What would have counted as failure?} A proposed new mapping would be rejected if it broke functionality, missed required delay targets without a compensating purpose, or exhausted permitted resources. An engineering refactor would not qualify as an area-preserving speedup merely because it was faster on one selected case: it had to retain complete official results and survive paired and additional-function checks. These tests do not prove hidden-case performance, but they make the research decisions falsifiable.
 
 \section{Starting point: what is the real synthesis problem?}
 A complete truth table for $F:\{0,1\}^n\to\{0,1\}^m$ contains $2^n$ input assignments and $m$ output bits per row. One can always write a sum of minterms: for each row on which $f=1$, form the conjunction of the required input literals, then OR all such conjunctions. The construction is exact, but it can repeat the same combinations, use many NOT gates, and produce long chains of ANDs and ORs. This shows the central tension: \emph{existence of a legal circuit} is elementary, while finding a good shared circuit is not.
