 \section{Round 4.1: construct the first explicit Area--Runtime Pareto comparison}
+\textbf{Counterfactual considered.} We could continue adding variable orders or polarity restarts and keep every tiny public-case area improvement. But this would spend more of a finite synthesis budget on strategies whose extra benefit had become small. The alternative was not to abandon the improved R4 circuit: it was to ask how much area a faster search would actually sacrifice.
+
 The next experiment varies the amount of polarity postprocessing and variable-order exploration. It retains three executable configurations rather than presenting one parameter setting as universally best: \textbf{Strict} minimizes perturbation of the R4 circuit, \textbf{Balanced} accepts a tiny area change for runtime reduction, and \textbf{Fast} reduces the search further. These are meaningful \emph{Pareto alternatives}: lower runtime can require worse area, and a global criterion is impossible without knowing the actual scoring function.
 
 Two WSL measurements report that the Balanced configuration produces Area 529,014 in both runs; its median runtime is 96.048 and 102.615 ms. Strict preserves Area 528,910, while Fast reaches Area 530,822. In the case-level data, Fast has a conspicuous area regression in \texttt{case\_018}; the small relative area-sum penalty conceals a large localized change. This demonstrates why we record per-case data and not only aggregate sums.
