 \section{Round 5: remove recomputation before reducing search quality}
+\textbf{The change of object.} Until now, the algorithm had treated the circuit as the thing being optimized. Here the \emph{synthesis program} becomes the object of analysis. This is not a semantic change to a Boolean function: we are asking whether repeated work inside the compiler can be removed before sacrificing any useful gate mapping candidate.
 \subsection{A useful conceptual split}
 There are two ways to accelerate a synthesis engine: \emph{delete candidate searches}, or \emph{implement the same searches more efficiently}. The latter is preferable when we already trust the candidate quality. Profiling revealed three repeated operations: multiple mapping policies rebuild the same ordered BDD; several small-support candidates recompute identical implicant covers; and every candidate's textual netlist is reparsed for area and timing estimates.
 
