 \section{Round 5.1: treat the netlist as a data structure, not repeated text}
+\textbf{Why another implementation round?} Caching helped with BDDs and covers, but the candidates still passed through several text-to-graph and graph-to-text transformations. Those conversions have no Boolean significance; they exist only because of an inconvenient intermediate representation. Round 5.1 therefore tests whether a typed internal graph can retain the same transformation logic while making it cheaper to execute.
 \subsection{The engineering hypothesis}
 A text format is a good external interface but a poor internal representation for repeated graph operations. Instead of parsing a netlist for rebalancing, serializing it, then parsing again for area recovery, Round 5.1 stores a typed gate table $v\mapsto(\mathrm{op},a,b)$ and an explicit output list. Structural hashing interns identical immutable gates; reachability pruning discards dead nodes at the final serialization step. For commutative AND2/OR2 nodes the builder sorts input IDs before hash lookup. For rebalancing, a same-operation internal cone is flattened only when the interior nodes have a single consumer, while output references are included in the fanout count. This matters in a multi-output network: reusing an output as someone else's intermediate result must not cause that shared computation to be silently removed. A topological induction over gate IDs proves the functional equivalence of these associative, commutative and idempotent rewrites; a reverse reachability pass from \emph{all} output roots preserves every live shared gate.
 
