 \section{Round 6.0: a change of representation, not merely another BDD order}
+\textbf{Why resume Boolean optimization after engineering speedups?} A faster synthesis engine is valuable only if it gives room to seek better realizations, or if synthesis time itself is a scoring concern. The next experiment deliberately returns to gate quality: a mapped DAG can still contain locally expensive expressions that are not exposed by changing the original BDD order. This is a new hypothesis, not an extension of the caching theorem.
 \subsection{Motivation: escaped local equivalences}
 A BDD-derived circuit may already be compact as a BDD yet still contain local multi-gate patterns that another circuit can implement more cheaply. The new idea is to inspect \emph{the final gate DAG itself}, choose a small boundary of signals, recover the exact Boolean subfunction between that boundary and an internal output, and resynthesize the subfunction independently of the original local shape.
 
