 \section{A consolidated evidence narrative}
+\subsection{The useful negative results}
+Not every valid rewrite should be adopted. In the present study, a large truth-table-derived candidate pool could become impractically expensive; phase/algebra refinements produced diminishing public-case area gains; the smaller generated cases sometimes showed negligible runtime benefit from typed DAGs; and four-feasible cut rewriting reduced area but breached the local runtime-acceptance threshold. These observations are not defects to omit from a research record. Each separates a \emph{correct mathematical transform} from a \emph{justified engineering decision}. They also explain why further tuning of a public 25-case benchmark was stopped.
+
+A particularly important counterfactual is the decision \emph{not} to promote R6.1. Had we selected the default solely by public-case area sum, we would have ignored its roughly 47.7\% local median-runtime penalty relative to R5.1. Had we dismissed it solely because of that penalty, we would have missed the independent evidence that cut rewriting can help other generated function families. Preserving the branch as an experimental result is more informative than either extreme.
+
