 \section{Reflection and next steps}
+\textbf{Research lesson.} A mathematical identity can certify equivalence; a cost model determines whether the identity is economically useful; a benchmark reports how a particular implementation behaved; and an adoption rule explains why that behavior warranted changing the default. Treating these as separate levels of reasoning is the central habit this project developed. The largest early improvements came from changing the search representation and acknowledging global timing; the later rounds taught us when \emph{not} to trade a large amount of computation for a tiny area gain.
+
 The project begins with a basic identity from discrete mathematics, but the practical problem quickly becomes one of \emph{shared structure, global constraints and resource-aware search}. The most decisive insight was not a particular identity by itself. It was learning when the relevant object changed: from a Boolean function, to an ROBDD of shared cofactors, to a priced physical gate DAG, to a deadline-constrained graph, and finally to an algorithm that consumes computation and memory while exploring candidate graphs.
 
 An area-optimization algorithm can be mathematically sound and still be the wrong engineering choice when its marginal improvement is dwarfed by synthesis time. Preserving that negative result demonstrates an appropriate stopping criterion, especially under a rubric that rewards deep understanding and justified conceptual application. We therefore freeze Round 5.1 as the principal Stage 1 engineering baseline and preserve Rounds 6.0/6.1 as exploration branches, subject to the students' final submission choice.
